  {
    "episode_id": 71430,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71430.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "That's right. This is, you know, most of the you'll see on most of the new it's a $900 billion package. Everyone that received the stimulus of $1,200 in the spring will now get $600 this time. And the purpose of that is to help Main Street to get more money in people's pockets, certainly for those people that need it, but also even beyond that to get people money in people's pockets so they can go out to Main Street and spend at the local stores or local restaurants and help the businesses that are struggling so much. You know, we've got Warp Speed is now rolling out the vaccine. That's the good news there. There's funding in this bill. for hospitals and first responders, additional funds. But we've got plenty of dollars there, I think, to do whatever needs to be done in getting the vaccine out to people quickly. This CARES Act is a little more focused on the impact of COVID on the economy, first responders, and other things. And if I can, maybe go through some of the detail that you probably won't hear about on DTV, but 501c organizations, nonprofits will be able to get PPP loans. The PPP loan has been extended. And if your business has really suffered, like we know so many restaurants and so many entertainment folks.",
